<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medieval Inquisition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1230s–15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Papal Inquisition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> was a series of papal tribunals established from the 1230s to combat heresy, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> and Waldensians of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Staffed mainly by Dominican and Franciscan friars, it questioned suspects, recorded testimony and handed the unrepentant to secular authorities for punishment. Its work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> helped destroy Catharism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medieval-inquisition.jpg" alt="Medieval Inquisition" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_IX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
